{
  "architecturalPlan": {
    "scaleRationale": "A $25 million oceanfront residence is expressed as a highly resolved, compact estate rather than a sprawling compound: approximately 792 m² enclosed over two levels, with a dramatic six-metre salon, substantial entertaining rooms, three private upper suites, service accommodation, roof terraces, a shaded ocean loggia and a 72 m² pool. The investment is concentrated in generous spatial volume, deep shade, large operable glazed openings, limestone structure, bronze detailing and crafted timber soffits rather than excessive enclosed area.",
    "levels": 2,
    "enclosedAreaM2": 792,
    "arrivalAndCirculation": "Arrival is from the landward north into a sheltered entrance gallery. A central axial gallery leads south into the principal salon and through to the ocean loggia, pool and sea horizon. The upper level is represented by supported floor plates over the salon; internal stairs are intentionally not modeled because stair geometry is unsupported by the current primitives.",
    "volumeHierarchy": "The primary volume is a tall, limestone-framed central salon under the highest flat roof datum. Lower east and west pavilions form dining/kitchen and lounge/service counterparts. The upper private level is nested within the principal volume, while low side-wing roofs become usable outlook terraces.",
    "relationshipsAndViews": "The salon, dining room and west lounge all open toward the south-facing ocean loggia. The arrival gallery is deliberately more solid and shaded on the north side. Upper bedroom volumes overlook the pool and ocean, while side roof terraces face the water over the low wings.",
    "roofFacadeAndMaterials": "A layered composition of deep flat roofs gives the residence a contemporary tropical profile. Ivory limestone walls and piers provide thermal mass; recessed bronze glazing faces the ocean; timber screens and soffits make shade tactile. Side-wing roofs sit lower than the central salon roof and establish protected outdoor terraces.",
